#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186216937 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "57c0a1cb-fb61-52f6-a966-f47b0984daa0" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Riccati Differential Equations"}] :question/id #uuid "7ad71ab7-0054-5b72-b82b-5a1010be8e09" :question/math-academy-id "e-21079" :question/problem "Which of the following equations is a Riccati differential equation?\n\n- $y' = 4xy^2 - 3y + 1$\n\n- $y' - 2y = (x^2 + 5)y^2 + \\sin(x)$\n\n- $y' = e^xy^3 + y - x$" :question/worked-solution "A first-order differential equation is a Riccati differential equation if it can be written in the form\n\n$$\ny' = a(x)y^2 + b(x)y + c(x).\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine the given options.\n\n- Equation I is a Riccati differential equation with\n\n$$\na(x) = 4x, \\qquad b(x)=-3, \\qquad c(x)=1.\n$$\n\n- Equation II is a Riccati differential equation. Writing the equation in standard form, we get\n\n$$\ny' = (x^2 + 5)y^2 + \\sin(x) + 2y.\n$$\n\nThis is a Riccati differential equation with\n\n$$\na(x) = x^2 + 5, \\qquad b(x)=2, \\qquad c(x)=\\sin(x).\n$$\n\n- Equation III is not a Riccati differential equation. Writing the equation in standard form, we get\n\n$$\ny' = e^xy^3 + y - x.\n$$\n\nThis is not a Riccati differential equation since it includes a $y^3$ term.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186216938 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "0c5fc503-d0bb-54e3-8192-dbb30aef1d27" :knowledge-point/title "Reducing a Riccati Differential Equation to a Bernoulli Equation"}] :question/id #uuid "97fae947-db0f-55bd-9b9c-4095ff0f64a4" :question/math-academy-id "e-21080" :question/problem "The function $y_0(x) = 1$ is a particular solution of the Riccati differential equation\n\n$$\ny' = 3y^2 - 7y + 4.\n$$\n\nExpress this equation in the Bernoulli form\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\textrm{d}z}{\\textrm{d}x} + P(x)z = Q(x)z^n\n$$\n\nand state the functions $P(x)$ and $Q(x),$ and the value of $n.$" :question/worked-solution "A Riccati differential equation has the form\n\n$$\ny' = a(x)y^2 + b(x)y + c(x).\n$$\n\nTo reduce the given Riccati equation to a Bernoulli equation, we use the substitution\n\n$$\ny(x) = z(x) + y_0(x),\n$$\n\nwhere $y_0(x)$ is a known particular solution.\nDifferentiating gives\n\n$$\ny'(x) = z'(x) + y_0'(x).\n$$\n\nIn our case, $y_0(x) = 1.$ Since $y_0'(x) = 0$, we have\n\n$$\ny = z + 1 \\qquad \\Rightarrow \\qquad y' = z'.\n$$\n\nLet's substitute these expressions into our Riccati equation:\n\n$$\nz' = 3(z + 1)^2 - 7(z + 1) + 4.\n$$\n\nNow, we expand the right-hand side, and simplify:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{z}^{′} & =3(z+1)^{2}-7(z+1)+4 \\\\ {z}^{′} & =3{z}^{2}-z \\\\ {z}^{′}+z & =3{z}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis is a Bernoulli equation of the form\n\n$$\n\\frac{ \\textrm{d} z}{\\textrm{d}x} + P(x)z = Q(x)z^n\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n$$\nP(x) = 1, \\qquad \nQ(x) = 3, \\qquad \nn = 2.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186216939 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "cb23c291-a2fb-5e57-bbc9-7b2c02d0a1ea" :knowledge-point/title "Solving Riccati Differential Equations"}] :question/id #uuid "d9bbb25d-0622-5b36-bcfa-a8375e326576" :question/math-academy-id "e-21081" :question/problem "Given that $y_0(x) = 4x$ is a particular solution of the Riccati differential equation\n\n$$\ny' = 2x\\,y^2 - 16x^2 y + 32x^3 + 4\n$$\n\nfind the general solution of the equation." :question/worked-solution "The equation\n\n$$\ny' = 2x\\,y^2 - 16x^2 y + 32x^3 + 4,\n$$\n\nis a Riccati differential equation of the form\n\n$$\ny' = a(x)y^2 + b(x)y + c(x),\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n$$\na(x) = 2x, \\qquad b(x) = -16x^2, \\qquad c(x) = 32x^3 + 4.\n$$\n\nTo reduce the given Riccati equation to a Bernoulli equation, we use the substitution\n\n$$\ny(x) = z(x) + y_0(x),\n$$\n\nwhere $y_0(x)$ is a known particular solution.\nDifferentiating gives\n\n$$\ny'(x) = z'(x) + y_0'(x).\n$$\n\nIn our case, $y_0(x) = 4x.$ Since $y_0'(x) = 4$, we have\n\n$$\ny = z + 4x \\qquad \\Rightarrow \\qquad y' = z' + 4.\n$$\n\nLet's substitute these expressions into our Riccati equation:\n\n$$\nz' + 4 = 2x\\,(z + 4x)^2 - 16x^2 (z + 4x) + 32x^3 + 4.\n$$\n\nNow, we expand the right-hand side, and simplify:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{z}^{′}+4 & =2x({z}^{2}+8xz+16{x}^{2})-16{x}^{2}(z+4x)+32{x}^{3}+4 \\\\ {z}^{′}+4 & =2x{z}^{2}+4 \\\\ {z}^{′} & =2x{z}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can solve the obtained equation using separation of variables:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x} & =2x{z}^{2} \\\\ \\frac{1}{{z}^{2}}\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x} & =2x \\\\ \\int \\frac{1}{{z}^{2}}\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int 2x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ \\int {z}^{-2}\\,\\text{d}z & =\\int 2x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ -\\frac{1}{z} & ={x}^{2}+C \\\\ z & =-\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}+C}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $C$ is an arbitrary constant.\n\nFinally, let's substitute $z = y-4x$ to recover the initial variable $y{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}z & =-\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}+C} \\\\ y-4x & =-\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}+C} \\\\ y & =-\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}+C}+4x \\\\  & =4x-\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}+C}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186217738 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "57c0a1cb-fb61-52f6-a966-f47b0984daa0" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Riccati Differential Equations"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "d7a1fe1d-221d-5c05-b40d-2f05b90e24da" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186216523} {:answer/id #uuid "e0057490-e9e6-55b0-8fe0-f699acd84c07" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186216524} {:answer/id #uuid "e2fa6476-305f-5498-ae1a-219897b54fd6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186216525} {:answer/id #uuid "3b59a381-b1f6-570d-bd0f-7121172b7090" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186216526} {:answer/id #uuid "54450020-0e39-561a-804a-79f86a32e3c4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186216527}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e2fa6476-305f-5498-ae1a-219897b54fd6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186216525} :answer-field/id #uuid "424cc51c-827f-58e3-b26a-23d89ae1df45" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186216522}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "41edd4a5-308f-5a31-bafd-5300fefbb7ea" :question/math-academy-id "q-333306" :question/problem "Which of the following equations is a Riccati differential equation?\n\n- ${y}^{′}={y}^{3}-2xy+4$\n- ${y}^{′}=6x{y}^{2}+3y-x$\n- ${y}^{′}+5y=2{x}^{2}{y}^{2}+7$" :question/worked-solution "A first-order differential equation is a Riccati differential equation if it can be written in the form\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=a(x){y}^{2}+b(x)y+c(x).\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine the given options.\n\n- Equation I is not a Riccati differential equation. Writing the equation in standard form, we get\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}={y}^{3}-2xy+4.\n$$\n\nThis is not a Riccati differential equation since it includes a ${y}^{3}$ term.\n\n- Equation II is a Riccati differential equation with\n\n$$\na(x)=6x,\\,b(x)=3,\\,c(x)=-x.\n$$\n\n- Equation III is a Riccati differential equation. Writing the equation in standard form, we get\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=2{x}^{2}{y}^{2}+7-5y.\n$$\n\nThis is a Riccati differential equation with\n\n$$\na(x)=2{x}^{2},\\,b(x)=-5,\\,c(x)=7.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186331546 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "57c0a1cb-fb61-52f6-a966-f47b0984daa0" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Riccati Differential Equations"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "accd7c10-6320-5282-b2dc-ae7e902b3d80" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186331548} {:answer/id #uuid "dd2eb207-452f-58b3-9db6-d9ccedd71138" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186331549} {:answer/id #uuid "8bfdab07-078f-5f04-943b-71857df51fae" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186331550} {:answer/id #uuid "ff09cf67-10e4-5716-abc6-4037e116060e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186331551} {:answer/id #uuid "dc253216-7879-555c-96d5-aaf941ceab4b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186331552}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "dd2eb207-452f-58b3-9db6-d9ccedd71138" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186331549} :answer-field/id #uuid "7ac5fd01-0245-55d3-8361-d40f715b1bbe" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186331547}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "94127b29-55de-5199-8276-7f5854f6b819" :question/math-academy-id "q-333311" :question/problem "Which of the following equations is a Riccati differential equation?\n\n- ${y}^{′}=({x}^{2}+1){y}^{2}-3y+x$\n- ${y}^{′}=x{y}^{3}+2y-1$\n- $y{y}^{′}={x}^{2}{y}^{2}+5$" :question/worked-solution "A first-order differential equation is a Riccati differential equation if it can be written in the form\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=a(x){y}^{2}+b(x)y+c(x).\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine the given options.\n\n- Equation I is a Riccati differential equation with \n\n$$\na(x)={x}^{2}+1,\\,b(x)=-3,\\,c(x)=x.\n$$\n\n- Equation II is not a Riccati differential equation. Writing the equation in standard form, we get\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=x{y}^{3}+2y-1\n$$\n\nThis is not a Riccati differential equation since it includes a ${y}^{3}$ term.\n\n- Equation III is not a Riccati differential equation. Writing the equation in standard form, we get\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}={x}^{2}y+\\frac{5}{y}.\n$$\n\nThis is not a Riccati differential equation because it contains a $\\frac{5}{y}$ term instead of a combination involving only ${y}^{2}$, $y$, and a function of $x.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186331553 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "57c0a1cb-fb61-52f6-a966-f47b0984daa0" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Riccati Differential Equations"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2afa58ce-c661-5806-b0ca-e7873698c701" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186331555} {:answer/id #uuid "f1dc449b-7ff9-5aa9-8d57-fbcc8a891798" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186331556} {:answer/id #uuid "f0477893-a944-5500-b568-86a5c417cea8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186331557} {:answer/id #uuid "a39671f7-edab-544e-a728-a76a0517ba0e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186331558} {:answer/id #uuid "12fff4fe-08f0-564c-9537-fef37bf91006" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186331559}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f0477893-a944-5500-b568-86a5c417cea8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186331557} :answer-field/id #uuid "62366818-b590-5703-b142-e5af78439d2d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186331554}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "e13e3bde-ee91-5c94-b3a9-45ec499c5457" :question/math-academy-id "q-333316" :question/problem "Which of the following equations is a Riccati differential equation?\n\n- ${y}^{′}={y}^{3}-2xy+5$\n- $y{y}^{′}={x}^{2}{y}^{2}+4$\n- ${y}^{′}-4y=(x-2){y}^{2}+{e}^{x}$" :question/worked-solution "A first-order differential equation is a Riccati differential equation if it can be written in the form\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=a(x){y}^{2}+b(x)y+c(x).\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine the given options.\n\n- Equation I is not a Riccati differential equation. Writing the equation in standard form, we get\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}={y}^{3}-2xy+5.\n$$\n\nThis is not a Riccati differential equation since it includes a ${y}^{3}$ term.\n\n- Equation II is not a Riccati differential equation. Writing the equation in standard form, we get\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}={x}^{2}y+\\frac{4}{y}.\n$$\n\nThis is not a Riccati differential equation because it contains a $\\frac{4}{y}$ term instead of a combination involving only ${y}^{2}$, $y$, and a function of $x.$\n\n- Equation III is a Riccati differential equation. Writing the equation in standard form, we get \n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=(x-2){y}^{2}+{e}^{x}+4y.\n$$\n\nThis is a Riccati differential equation with\n\n$$\na(x)=x-2,\\,b(x)=4,\\,c(x)={e}^{x}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186331560 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "57c0a1cb-fb61-52f6-a966-f47b0984daa0" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Riccati Differential Equations"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5cc20a41-cc55-5a7e-9770-3f8d6e23a40c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186331562} {:answer/id #uuid "d3d65aeb-ff47-5149-b3d6-af5efd748d8d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186331563} {:answer/id #uuid "84aaecd8-17c7-5b46-9fc3-30f7f3f31380" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186331564} {:answer/id #uuid "798e0fae-f175-5336-93fd-a676c889937e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186331565} {:answer/id #uuid "394eae98-39b4-515d-82df-4bc264a8c3b0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186331566}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "394eae98-39b4-515d-82df-4bc264a8c3b0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186331566} :answer-field/id #uuid "d2c934d1-f94f-56fa-8f01-50b98a5a7cc5" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186331561}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "49c14f9e-577f-57c2-8edd-a8fc8bbd3d8b" :question/math-academy-id "q-333321" :question/problem "Which of the following equations is a Riccati differential equation?\n\n- ${y}^{′}=2{y}^{3}-xy+1$\n- ${y}^{′}=({x}^{2}+3){y}^{2}-4y+{e}^{x}$\n- ${y}^{′}={x}^{2}y+\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x)}{y}$" :question/worked-solution "A first-order differential equation is a Riccati differential equation if it can be written in the form\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=a(x){y}^{2}+b(x)y+c(x).\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine the given options.\n\n- Equation I is not a Riccati differential equation. Writing the equation in standard form, we get\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=2{y}^{3}-xy+1.\n$$\n\nThis is not a Riccati differential equation since it includes a ${y}^{3}$ term.\n\n- Equation II is a Riccati differential equation with\n\n$$\na(x)={x}^{2}+3,\\,b(x)=-4,\\,c(x)={e}^{x}.\n$$\n\n- Equation III is not a Riccati differential equation. Writing the equation in standard form, we get\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}={x}^{2}y+\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x)}{y}.\n$$\n\nThis is not a Riccati differential equation because it contains a $\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x)}{y}$ term instead of a combination involving only ${y}^{2}$, $y$, and a function of $x$.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186331567 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "57c0a1cb-fb61-52f6-a966-f47b0984daa0" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Riccati Differential Equations"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "f423ca16-b8b2-5795-ae6d-d08febb11d4a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186331569} {:answer/id #uuid "15c3a524-94a5-57f1-987f-0e47739a369b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186331570} {:answer/id #uuid "b992e255-06e2-5238-859a-8144614449a0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186331571} {:answer/id #uuid "e9b06702-8bcc-517f-a05f-350ad04d55a5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186331572} {:answer/id #uuid "385c3ed0-7ea5-5850-9416-d9427d454f05" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186331573}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "15c3a524-94a5-57f1-987f-0e47739a369b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186331570} :answer-field/id #uuid "9a909c44-ca4c-532d-b422-b7bddb8f3da5" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186331568}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "d5cb8f47-e363-5362-ba33-237bf452c52c" :question/math-academy-id "q-333322" :question/problem "Which of the following equations is a Riccati differential equation?\n\n- ${y}^{′}=(x-1){y}^{2}+2xy+{x}^{2}$\n- ${y}^{′}={x}^{2}{y}^{3}-y+{e}^{x}$\n- $y{y}^{′}=({x}^{2}+1){y}^{2}-3$" :question/worked-solution "A first-order differential equation is a Riccati differential equation if it can be written in the form\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=a(x){y}^{2}+b(x)y+c(x).\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine the given options.\n\n- Equation I is a Riccati differential equation with\n\n$$\na(x)=x-1,\\,b(x)=2x,\\,c(x)={x}^{2}.\n$$\n\n- Equation II is not a Riccati differential equation. Writing the equation in standard form, we get\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}={x}^{2}{y}^{3}-y+{e}^{x}.\n$$\n\nThis is not a Riccati differential equation since it includes a ${y}^{3}$ term.\n\n- Equation III is not a Riccati differential equation.Writing the equation in standard form, we get\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=({x}^{2}+1)y-\\frac{3}{y}.\n$$\n\nThis is not a Riccati differential equation because it contains a $\\frac{3}{y}$ term instead of a combination involving only ${y}^{2}$, $y$, and a function of $x.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186217739 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "0c5fc503-d0bb-54e3-8192-dbb30aef1d27" :knowledge-point/title "Reducing a Riccati Differential Equation to a Bernoulli Equation"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "56f891a3-54de-549b-8f6a-6488cf4ffa99" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186331576}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "56f891a3-54de-549b-8f6a-6488cf4ffa99" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186331576} :answer-field/id #uuid "ae04aa79-961a-575a-9cd6-8279e3980e86" :answer-field/key "field-3" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186216528} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1543cb57-936b-5c5b-bd19-48b96afca359" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186331575}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "1543cb57-936b-5c5b-bd19-48b96afca359" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186331575} :answer-field/id #uuid "f6079969-02a4-5291-838f-5c62fa2b1cbb" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186216529} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "bfb7cfd6-b4af-5eff-922b-a28546ef57a2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186331574}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "bfb7cfd6-b4af-5eff-922b-a28546ef57a2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186331574} :answer-field/id #uuid "25520bf5-ddf9-5014-8522-6b3884046d75" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186216530}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "97143866-a7bb-5489-993f-170b6d862f7d" :question/math-academy-id "q-333607" :question/problem "The function ${y}_{0}(x)=1$ is a particular solution of the Riccati differential equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}={y}^{2}-3y+2.\n$$\n\nExpress this equation in the Bernoulli form\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}+P(x)z=Q(x){z}^{n}\n$$\n\nand state the functions $P(x)$ and $Q(x),$ and the value of $n.$\n\n$P(x)=$\n{{field-1}}\n$,\\,Q(x)=$\n{{field-2}}\n$,\\,n=$\n{{field-3}}" :question/worked-solution "A Riccati differential equation has the form\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=a(x){y}^{2}+b(x)y+c(x).\n$$\n\nTo reduce the given Riccati equation to a Bernoulli equation, we use the substitution\n\n$$\ny(x)=z(x)+{y}_{0}(x),\n$$\n\nwhere ${y}_{0}(x)$ is a known particular solution.\nDifferentiating gives\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}(x)={z}^{′}(x)+{y}_{0}^{′}(x).\n$$\n\nIn our case, ${y}_{0}(x)=1.$ Since ${y}_{0}^{′}(x)=0$, we have\n\n$$\ny=z+1\\,⇒\\,{y}^{′}={z}^{′}.\n$$\n\nLet's substitute these expressions into our Riccati equation:\n\n$$\n{z}^{′}=(z+1)^{2}-3(z+1)+2.\n$$\n\nNow, we expand the right-hand side, and simplify:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{z}^{′} & =(z+1)^{2}-3(z+1)+2 \\\\ {z}^{′} & ={z}^{2}-z \\\\ {z}^{′}+z & ={z}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis is a Bernoulli equation of the form\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}+P(x)z=Q(x){z}^{n}\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n$$\nP(x)=1,\\,Q(x)=1,\\,n=2.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186331577 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "0c5fc503-d0bb-54e3-8192-dbb30aef1d27" :knowledge-point/title "Reducing a Riccati Differential Equation to a Bernoulli Equation"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "f75fc82d-d106-52c0-8d75-f9cb5e892972" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-x-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186331579}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f75fc82d-d106-52c0-8d75-f9cb5e892972" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-x-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186331579} :answer-field/id #uuid "1277533d-8e93-5b7b-aadc-c33b156181a4" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186331578} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c4fd329c-5f5b-5b04-9425-5f3e066d7cb1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x+1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186331581}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c4fd329c-5f5b-5b04-9425-5f3e066d7cb1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x+1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186331581} :answer-field/id #uuid "881323ae-4d34-5f11-9c8c-db70334e4908" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186331580} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "e1fa0788-4deb-5af5-b868-e11f8f8a183d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186331583}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e1fa0788-4deb-5af5-b868-e11f8f8a183d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186331583} :answer-field/id #uuid "3c437494-168d-5d86-be3c-2a1ecbc9be81" :answer-field/key "field-3" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186331582}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "c48273b1-7d55-5f8f-aa33-20c2e19dfbbf" :question/math-academy-id "q-333781" :question/problem "The function ${y}_{0}(x)=1$ is a particular solution of the Riccati differential equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=(x+1){y}^{2}-xy-1.\n$$\n\nExpress this equation in the Bernoulli form\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}+P(x)z=Q(x){z}^{n}\n$$\n\nand state the functions $P(x)$ and $Q(x),$ and the value of $n.$\n\n$P(x)=$\n{{field-1}}\n$,\\,Q(x)=$\n{{field-2}}\n$,\\,n=$\n{{field-3}}" :question/worked-solution "A Riccati differential equation has the form\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=a(x){y}^{2}+b(x)y+c(x).\n$$\n\nTo reduce the given Riccati equation to a Bernoulli equation, we use the substitution\n\n$$\ny(x)=z(x)+{y}_{0}(x),\n$$\n\nwhere ${y}_{0}(x)$ is a known particular solution.\nDifferentiating gives\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}(x)={z}^{′}(x)+{y}_{0}^{′}(x).\n$$\n\nIn our case, ${y}_{0}(x)=1.$ Since ${y}_{0}^{′}(x)=0$, we have\n\n$$\ny=z+1\\,⇒\\,{y}^{′}={z}^{′}.\n$$\n\nLet's substitute these expressions into our Riccati equation:\n\n$$\n{z}^{′}=(x+1)(z+1)^{2}-x(z+1)-1.\n$$\n\nNow, we expand the right-hand side, and simplify:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{z}^{′} & =(x+1)({z}^{2}+2z+1)-xz-x-1 \\\\ {z}^{′} & =(x+1){z}^{2}+(x+2)z \\\\ {z}^{′}-(x+2)z & =(x+1){z}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis is a Bernoulli equation of the form\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}+P(x)z=Q(x){z}^{n}\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n$$\nP(x)=-(x+2),\\,Q(x)=x+1,\\,n=2.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186331584 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "0c5fc503-d0bb-54e3-8192-dbb30aef1d27" :knowledge-point/title "Reducing a Riccati Differential Equation to a Bernoulli Equation"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "cc88a998-e3ad-519c-8968-8d3fb7f55832" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186331586} {:answer/id #uuid "80440513-8419-5814-a170-922b925c1641" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186331587}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "cc88a998-e3ad-519c-8968-8d3fb7f55832" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186331586} :answer-field/id #uuid "7700b710-1835-5803-afaf-7746ca19a71b" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186331585} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "e67e825d-0e56-5547-bb8d-a9aeeaf02bbf" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "e^{-x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186331589}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e67e825d-0e56-5547-bb8d-a9aeeaf02bbf" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "e^{-x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186331589} :answer-field/id #uuid "b18b2654-5dff-5a5d-b3fe-96b0568e6ec1" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186331588} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0e3d3250-3d54-5927-9d58-5c4dafdb62e3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186331591}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "0e3d3250-3d54-5927-9d58-5c4dafdb62e3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186331591} :answer-field/id #uuid "dda1c19c-7ebc-563a-8d28-17a3bda4402d" :answer-field/key "field-3" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186331590}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "e115d7d7-88c8-52c9-896b-4ab9e5ffac9f" :question/math-academy-id "q-333790" :question/problem "The function ${y}_{0}(x)={e}^{-x}$ is a particular solution of the Riccati differential equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}={e}^{-x}{y}^{2}-(x+2{e}^{-2x})y+(x{e}^{-x}-{e}^{-x}+{e}^{-3x}).\n$$\n\nExpress this equation in the Bernoulli form\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}+P(x)z=Q(x){z}^{n}\n$$\n\nand state the functions $P(x)$ and $Q(x),$ and the value of $n.$\n\n$P(x)=$\n{{field-1}}\n$,\\,Q(x)=$\n{{field-2}}\n$,\\,n=$\n{{field-3}}" :question/worked-solution "A Riccati differential equation has the form\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=a(x){y}^{2}+b(x)y+c(x).\n$$\n\nTo reduce the given Riccati equation to a Bernoulli equation, we use the substitution\n\n$$\ny(x)=z(x)+{y}_{0}(x),\n$$\n\nwhere ${y}_{0}(x)$ is a known particular solution.\nDifferentiating gives\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}(x)={z}^{′}(x)+{y}_{0}^{′}(x).\n$$\n\nIn our case, ${y}_{0}(x)={e}^{-x}.$ Since ${y}_{0}^{′}(x)=-{e}^{-x}$, we have\n\n$$\ny=z+{e}^{-x}\\,⇒\\,{y}^{′}={z}^{′}-{e}^{-x}.\n$$\n\nLet's substitute these expressions into our Riccati equation:\n\n$$\n{z}^{′}-{e}^{-x}={e}^{-x}(z+{e}^{-x})^{2}-(x+2{e}^{-2x})(z+{e}^{-x})+(x{e}^{-x}-{e}^{-x}+{e}^{-3x}).\n$$\n\nNow, we expand the right-hand side, and simplify:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{z}^{′}-{e}^{-x} & ={e}^{-x}(z+{e}^{-x})^{2}-(x+2{e}^{-2x})(z+{e}^{-x})+(x{e}^{-x}-{e}^{-x}+{e}^{-3x}) \\\\ {z}^{′}-{e}^{-x} & ={e}^{-x}{z}^{2}-xz-{e}^{-x} \\\\ {z}^{′}+xz & ={e}^{-x}{z}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis is a Bernoulli equation of the form\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}+P(x)z=Q(x){z}^{n}\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n$$\nP(x)=x,\\,Q(x)={e}^{-x},\\,n=2.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186331592 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "0c5fc503-d0bb-54e3-8192-dbb30aef1d27" :knowledge-point/title "Reducing a Riccati Differential Equation to a Bernoulli Equation"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c57e29c3-223c-5c28-9f9c-a77c2fafc5d7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186331594} {:answer/id #uuid "11564976-ef5b-55b3-9bc8-19823a171ab6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186331595}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "11564976-ef5b-55b3-9bc8-19823a171ab6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186331595} :answer-field/id #uuid "ce7e4adc-1a51-549f-8c89-cc44f07a02d1" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186331593} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "066de8a6-6d68-5106-8009-c64cbb0f868f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186331597}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "066de8a6-6d68-5106-8009-c64cbb0f868f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186331597} :answer-field/id #uuid "d3afad78-3149-5dd0-bd50-08e3b6fc7675" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186331596} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "887cf190-c419-5811-b8ce-e8dfbacc8f72" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186331599}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "887cf190-c419-5811-b8ce-e8dfbacc8f72" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186331599} :answer-field/id #uuid "23908abb-9d4f-5562-9511-325a5d4b07b4" :answer-field/key "field-3" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186331598}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "c3b03bd2-59be-5ed8-869e-08f8515b5974" :question/math-academy-id "q-333792" :question/problem "The function ${y}_{0}(x)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡x$ is a particular solution of the Riccati differential equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=4{y}^{2}-(8\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+4)y+(4{sin}^{2}⁡x+4\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+\\operatorname{cos}⁡x).\n$$\n\nExpress this equation in the Bernoulli form\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}+P(x)z=Q(x){z}^{n}\n$$\n\nand state the functions $P(x)$ and $Q(x),$ and the value of $n.$\n\n$P(x)=$\n{{field-1}}\n$,\\,Q(x)=$\n{{field-2}}\n$,\\,n=$\n{{field-3}}" :question/worked-solution "A Riccati differential equation has the form\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=a(x){y}^{2}+b(x)y+c(x).\n$$\n\nTo reduce the given Riccati equation to a Bernoulli equation, we use the substitution\n\n$$\ny(x)=z(x)+{y}_{0}(x),\n$$\n\nwhere ${y}_{0}(x)$ is a known particular solution.\nDifferentiating gives\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}(x)={z}^{′}(x)+{y}_{0}^{′}(x).\n$$\n\nIn our case, ${y}_{0}(x)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡x.$ Since ${y}_{0}^{′}(x)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡x$, we have\n\n$$\ny=z+\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\,⇒\\,{y}^{′}={z}^{′}+\\operatorname{cos}⁡x.\n$$\n\nLet's substitute these expressions into our Riccati equation:\n\n$$\n{z}^{′}+\\operatorname{cos}⁡x=4(z+\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)^{2}-(8\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+4)(z+\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)+(4{sin}^{2}⁡x+4\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+\\operatorname{cos}⁡x).\n$$\n\nNow, we expand the right-hand side, and simplify:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{z}^{′}+\\operatorname{cos}⁡x & =4(z+\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)^{2}-(8\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+4)(z+\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)+(4{sin}^{2}⁡x+4\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+\\operatorname{cos}⁡x) \\\\ {z}^{′}+\\operatorname{cos}⁡x & =4{z}^{2}-4z+\\operatorname{cos}⁡x \\\\ {z}^{′}+4z & =4{z}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis is a Bernoulli equation of the form\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}+P(x)z=Q(x){z}^{n}\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n$$\nP(x)=4,\\,Q(x)=4,\\,n=2.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186217740 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "cb23c291-a2fb-5e57-bbc9-7b2c02d0a1ea" :knowledge-point/title "Solving Riccati Differential Equations"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8e876a44-04d8-586e-8a6e-9276b967fef9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y = \\frac{2}{x} + \\frac{1}{x + C}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186216532} {:answer/id #uuid "047c2ef1-729e-5440-9d73-7bc16cbad0d2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y = \\frac{2}{x} - \\frac{1}{2x + C}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186216533} {:answer/id #uuid "166ea331-4854-530e-ae02-450a52b0c437" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y = \\frac{2}{x} - \\frac{1}{x + C}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186216534} {:answer/id #uuid "405ecbae-819f-5a22-a93b-37b7072a1182" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y = \\frac{2}{x} + \\frac{2}{x - C}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186216535} {:answer/id #uuid "6b400c9a-f042-57e1-80cb-5b27f353ca0a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y = \\frac{2}{x} + \\frac{2}{x + C}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186216536}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "166ea331-4854-530e-ae02-450a52b0c437" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y = \\frac{2}{x} - \\frac{1}{x + C}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186216534} :answer-field/id #uuid "f9bd62a2-882f-5ad3-b3e6-3af72b1a9d02" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186216531}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "c9b2868a-b882-5b68-b22b-3bc42a1c4dfc" :question/math-academy-id "q-333805" :question/problem "Given that ${y}_{0}(x)=\\frac{2}{x}$ is a particular solution of the Riccati differential equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}={y}^{2}-\\frac{4}{x}y+\\frac{2}{{x}^{2}}\n$$\n\n \n\nfind the general solution of the equation." :question/worked-solution "The equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}={y}^{2}-\\frac{4}{x}y+\\frac{2}{{x}^{2}},\n$$\n\nis a Riccati differential equation of the form\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=a(x){y}^{2}+b(x)y+c(x),\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n$$\na(x)=1,\\,b(x)=-\\frac{4}{x},\\,c(x)=\\frac{2}{{x}^{2}}.\n$$\n\nTo reduce the given Riccati equation to a Bernoulli equation, we use the substitution\n\n$$\ny(x)=z(x)+{y}_{0}(x),\n$$\n\nwhere ${y}_{0}(x)$ is a known particular solution.\nDifferentiating gives\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}(x)={z}^{′}(x)+{y}_{0}^{′}(x).\n$$\n\nIn our case, ${y}_{0}(x)=\\frac{2}{x}.$ Since ${y}_{0}^{′}(x)=-\\frac{2}{{x}^{2}}$, we have\n\n$$\ny=z+\\frac{2}{x}\\,⇒\\,{y}^{′}={z}^{′}-\\frac{2}{{x}^{2}}.\n$$\n\nLet's substitute these expressions into our Riccati equation:\n\n$$\n{z}^{′}-\\frac{2}{{x}^{2}}=(z+\\frac{2}{x})^{2}-\\frac{4}{x}(z+\\frac{2}{x})+\\frac{2}{{x}^{2}}\n$$\n\nNow, we expand the right-hand side, and simplify:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{z}^{′}-\\frac{2}{{x}^{2}} & =({z}^{2}+\\frac{4z}{x}+\\frac{4}{{x}^{2}})-(\\frac{4z}{x}+\\frac{8}{{x}^{2}})+\\frac{2}{{x}^{2}} \\\\ {z}^{′}-\\frac{2}{{x}^{2}} & ={z}^{2}-\\frac{2}{{x}^{2}} \\\\ {z}^{′} & ={z}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can solve the obtained equation using separation of variables:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x} & ={z}^{2} \\\\ \\frac{1}{{z}^{2}}\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x} & =1 \\\\ \\int \\frac{1}{{z}^{2}}\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int \\,\\text{d}x \\\\ \\int {z}^{-2}\\,\\text{d}z & =\\int \\text{d}x \\\\ -\\frac{1}{z} & =x+C \\\\ z & =-\\frac{1}{x+C}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $C$ is an arbitrary constant.\n\nFinally, let's substitute $z=y-\\frac{2}{x}$ to recover the initial variable $y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}z & =-\\frac{1}{x+C} \\\\ y-\\frac{2}{x} & =-\\frac{1}{x+C} \\\\ y & =-\\frac{1}{x+C}+\\frac{2}{x} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{x}-\\frac{1}{x+C}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186331600 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "0c5fc503-d0bb-54e3-8192-dbb30aef1d27" :knowledge-point/title "Reducing a Riccati Differential Equation to a Bernoulli Equation"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "80386ce1-bd09-5a0b-b0de-22a19348653c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186331602}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "80386ce1-bd09-5a0b-b0de-22a19348653c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186331602} :answer-field/id #uuid "3d64956b-7444-50a8-b4f5-4a7f768aeab5" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186331601} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "fc70e9ef-5a31-5763-94d8-cf5f7b53e01c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186331604}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "fc70e9ef-5a31-5763-94d8-cf5f7b53e01c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186331604} :answer-field/id #uuid "e2233501-9a7a-5dcf-92a9-5172eb7fd1e6" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186331603} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "098ea4d7-84af-57f1-91e0-ac8351b4f756" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186331606}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "098ea4d7-84af-57f1-91e0-ac8351b4f756" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186331606} :answer-field/id #uuid "19162ec1-2cf4-5324-a38e-77fcdb16405d" :answer-field/key "field-3" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186331605}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "0ac83712-c2f2-59cf-baec-1d70556d33fb" :question/math-academy-id "q-333902" :question/problem "The function ${y}_{0}(x)=2$ is a particular solution of the Riccati differential equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=4x\\,{y}^{2}-(16x+3)y+(16x+6).\n$$\n\nExpress this equation in the Bernoulli form\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}+P(x)z=Q(x){z}^{n}\n$$\n\nand state the functions $P(x)$ and $Q(x),$ and the value of $n.$\n\n$P(x)=$\n{{field-1}}\n$,\\,Q(x)=$\n{{field-2}}\n$,\\,n=$\n{{field-3}}" :question/worked-solution "A Riccati differential equation has the form\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=a(x){y}^{2}+b(x)y+c(x).\n$$\n\nTo reduce the given Riccati equation to a Bernoulli equation, we use the substitution\n\n$$\ny(x)=z(x)+{y}_{0}(x),\n$$\n\nwhere ${y}_{0}(x)$ is a known particular solution.\nDifferentiating gives\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}(x)={z}^{′}(x)+{y}_{0}^{′}(x).\n$$\n\nIn our case, ${y}_{0}(x)=2.$ Since ${y}_{0}^{′}(x)=0$, we have\n\n$$\ny=z+2\\,⇒\\,{y}^{′}={z}^{′}.\n$$\n\nLet's substitute these expressions into our Riccati equation:\n\n$$\n{z}^{′}=4x(z+2)^{2}-(16x+3)(z+2)+(16x+6).\n$$\n\nNow, we expand the right-hand side, and simplify:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{z}^{′} & =4x(z+2)^{2}-(16x+3)(z+2)+(16x+6) \\\\ {z}^{′} & =4x{z}^{2}-3z \\\\ {z}^{′}+3z & =4x{z}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis is a Bernoulli equation of the form\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}+P(x)z=Q(x){z}^{n}\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n$$\nP(x)=3,\\,Q(x)=4x,\\,n=2.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186331607 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "cb23c291-a2fb-5e57-bbc9-7b2c02d0a1ea" :knowledge-point/title "Solving Riccati Differential Equations"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "d126ec62-6504-5cd6-94c1-4c823bd7a85a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y={e}^{x}+\\frac{2}{{e}^{x}+C}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186331609} {:answer/id #uuid "9c354376-6b55-582f-87db-f713f7f9e5b7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y={e}^{x}+\\frac{2}{{e}^{x}-C}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186331610} {:answer/id #uuid "d71af105-5ce1-5757-80d5-8ed5bd63c67d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y={e}^{x}+\\frac{1}{{e}^{x}+C}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186331611} {:answer/id #uuid "9b664bf2-8764-589f-a443-6e8a7d17f594" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y={e}^{x}-\\frac{1}{{e}^{x}+C}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186331612} {:answer/id #uuid "e402141c-6190-5bd8-b1cb-7dd37debd2e0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y={e}^{x}-\\frac{1}{2{e}^{x}+C}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186331613}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "9b664bf2-8764-589f-a443-6e8a7d17f594" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y={e}^{x}-\\frac{1}{{e}^{x}+C}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186331612} :answer-field/id #uuid "63ea368c-104c-504b-b184-cd6f46cc7992" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186331608}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "de3e625d-cc13-5143-bb10-0064a034bf2d" :question/math-academy-id "q-333903" :question/problem "Given that ${y}_{0}(x)={e}^{x}$ is a particular solution of the Riccati differential equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}={e}^{x}{y}^{2}-2{e}^{2x}y+{e}^{3x}+{e}^{x}\n$$\n\n \n\nfind the general solution of the equation." :question/worked-solution "The equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}={e}^{x}{y}^{2}-2{e}^{2x}y+{e}^{3x}+{e}^{x},\n$$\n\nis a Riccati differential equation of the form\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=a(x){y}^{2}+b(x)y+c(x),\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n$$\na(x)={e}^{x},\\,b(x)=-2{e}^{2x},\\,c(x)={e}^{3x}+{e}^{x}.\n$$\n\nTo reduce the given Riccati equation to a Bernoulli equation, we use the substitution\n\n$$\ny(x)=z(x)+{y}_{0}(x),\n$$\n\nwhere ${y}_{0}(x)$ is a known particular solution.\nDifferentiating gives\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}(x)={z}^{′}(x)+{y}_{0}^{′}(x).\n$$\n\nIn our case, ${y}_{0}(x)={e}^{x}.$ Since ${y}_{0}^{′}(x)={e}^{x}$, we have\n\n$$\ny=z+{e}^{x}\\,⇒\\,{y}^{′}={z}^{′}+{e}^{x}.\n$$\n\nLet's substitute these expressions into our Riccati equation:\n\n$$\n{z}^{′}+{e}^{x}={e}^{x}(z+{e}^{x})^{2}-2{e}^{2x}(z+{e}^{x})+{e}^{3x}+{e}^{x}.\n$$\n\nNow, we expand the right-hand side, and simplify:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{z}^{′}+{e}^{x} & ={e}^{x}({z}^{2}+2{e}^{x}z+{e}^{2x})-2{e}^{2x}(z+{e}^{x})+{e}^{3x}+{e}^{x} \\\\ {z}^{′}+{e}^{x} & ={e}^{x}{z}^{2}+{e}^{x} \\\\ {z}^{′} & ={e}^{x}{z}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can solve the obtained equation using separation of variables:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x} & ={e}^{x}{z}^{2} \\\\ \\frac{1}{{z}^{2}}\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x} & ={e}^{x} \\\\ \\int \\frac{1}{{z}^{2}}\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int {e}^{x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ \\int {z}^{-2}\\,\\text{d}z & =\\int {e}^{x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ -\\frac{1}{z} & ={e}^{x}+C \\\\ z & =-\\frac{1}{{e}^{x}+C}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $C$ is an arbitrary constant.\n\nFinally, let's substitute $z=y-{e}^{x}$ to recover the initial variable $y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}z & =-\\frac{1}{{e}^{x}+C} \\\\ y-{e}^{x} & =-\\frac{1}{{e}^{x}+C} \\\\ y & =-\\frac{1}{{e}^{x}+C}+{e}^{x} \\\\  & ={e}^{x}-\\frac{1}{{e}^{x}+C}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186331614 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "cb23c291-a2fb-5e57-bbc9-7b2c02d0a1ea" :knowledge-point/title "Solving Riccati Differential Equations"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "04333c63-2e58-5790-bbc9-6d950fdedbcd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=x+1-\\frac{1}{x+C}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186331616} {:answer/id #uuid "933a72ae-2a44-586c-a6cd-a8a6b5e6f7b7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=x+1+\\frac{2}{3x-C}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186331617} {:answer/id #uuid "dbc84afc-2228-5d51-8e4f-6ba1fb8b790e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=x+1+\\frac{2}{3x+C}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186331618} {:answer/id #uuid "56c2bee2-34db-5d8d-94b5-d2e1238a2d9d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=x+1+\\frac{1}{3x+C}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186331619} {:answer/id #uuid "5c4cdcd4-7d8d-5486-aaff-c9656ecadcba" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=x+1-\\frac{1}{3x+C}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186331620}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "5c4cdcd4-7d8d-5486-aaff-c9656ecadcba" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=x+1-\\frac{1}{3x+C}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186331620} :answer-field/id #uuid "0646cb5c-b420-58ba-b062-5b5048bffc35" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186331615}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "ec40b5fc-8430-5d44-8beb-1e0c0c65c606" :question/math-academy-id "q-334096" :question/problem "Given that ${y}_{0}(x)=x+1$ is a particular solution of the Riccati differential equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=3{y}^{2}-6(x+1)y+3{x}^{2}+6x+4\n$$\n\n \n\nfind the general solution of the equation." :question/worked-solution "The equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=3{y}^{2}-6(x+1)y+3{x}^{2}+6x+4,\n$$\n\nis a Riccati differential equation of the form\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=a(x){y}^{2}+b(x)y+c(x),\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n$$\na(x)=3,\\,b(x)=-6(x+1),\\,c(x)=3{x}^{2}+6x+4.\n$$\n\nTo reduce the given Riccati equation to a Bernoulli equation, we use the substitution\n\n$$\ny(x)=z(x)+{y}_{0}(x),\n$$\n\nwhere ${y}_{0}(x)$ is a known particular solution.\nDifferentiating gives\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}(x)={z}^{′}(x)+{y}_{0}^{′}(x).\n$$\n\nIn our case, ${y}_{0}(x)=x+1.$ Since ${y}_{0}^{′}(x)=1$, we have\n\n$$\ny=z+x+1\\,⇒\\,{y}^{′}={z}^{′}+1.\n$$\n\nLet's substitute these expressions into our Riccati equation:\n\n$$\n{z}^{′}+1=3(z+x+1)^{2}-6(x+1)(z+x+1)+3{x}^{2}+6x+4.\n$$\n\nNow, we expand the right-hand side, and simplify:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{z}^{′}+1 & =3((x+1)^{2}+2(x+1)z+{z}^{2})-6(x+1)(z+x+1)+3{x}^{2}+6x+4 \\\\ {z}^{′}+1 & =3{z}^{2}+1 \\\\ {z}^{′} & =3{z}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can solve the obtained equation using separation of variables:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x} & =3{z}^{2} \\\\ \\frac{1}{{z}^{2}}\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x} & =3 \\\\ \\int \\frac{1}{{z}^{2}}\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int 3\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ \\int {z}^{-2}\\,\\text{d}z & =\\int 3\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ -\\frac{1}{z} & =3x+C \\\\ z & =-\\frac{1}{3x+C}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $C$ is an arbitrary constant.\n\nFinally, let's substitute $z=y-(x+1)$ to recover the initial variable $y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}z & =-\\frac{1}{3x+C} \\\\ y-(x+1) & =-\\frac{1}{3x+C} \\\\ y & =-\\frac{1}{3x+C}+x+1 \\\\  & =x+1-\\frac{1}{3x+C}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186331621 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "cb23c291-a2fb-5e57-bbc9-7b2c02d0a1ea" :knowledge-point/title "Solving Riccati Differential Equations"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c9effa7a-adad-5d88-998f-cfaf59bfaf24" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+\\frac{2}{{x}^{2}+C}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186331623} {:answer/id #uuid "d87b644b-2396-5580-a1f8-f6b2f9cef910" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=\\operatorname{cos}⁡x-\\frac{1}{2{x}^{2}+C}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186331624} {:answer/id #uuid "f05ecd86-ee83-5c4d-b1cb-6f7ee94da922" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+\\frac{2}{{x}^{2}-C}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186331625} {:answer/id #uuid "7c446e51-99ae-5541-8384-f2752f7d9895" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=\\operatorname{cos}⁡x-\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}+C}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186331626} {:answer/id #uuid "0608c634-459c-5a61-b9dd-14aade7352d6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}+C}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186331627}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "7c446e51-99ae-5541-8384-f2752f7d9895" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=\\operatorname{cos}⁡x-\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}+C}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186331626} :answer-field/id #uuid "59b2bf6d-557b-5d74-87fe-35d8ad0a30e0" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186331622}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "cfdc15b3-f049-5c54-b917-bb040b11bf90" :question/math-academy-id "q-334122" :question/problem "Given that ${y}_{0}(x)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡x$ is a particular solution of the Riccati differential equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=2x\\,{y}^{2}-4x\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\,y+2x{cos}^{2}⁡x-\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\n$$\n\n \n\nfind the general solution of the equation." :question/worked-solution "The equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=2x\\,{y}^{2}-4x\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\,y+2x{cos}^{2}⁡x-\\operatorname{sin}⁡x,\n$$\n\nis a Riccati differential equation of the form\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=a(x){y}^{2}+b(x)y+c(x),\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n$$\na(x)=2x,\\,b(x)=-4x\\operatorname{cos}⁡x,\\,c(x)=2x{cos}^{2}⁡x-\\operatorname{sin}⁡x.\n$$\n\nTo reduce the given Riccati equation to a Bernoulli equation, we use the substitution\n\n$$\ny(x)=z(x)+{y}_{0}(x),\n$$\n\nwhere ${y}_{0}(x)$ is a known particular solution.\nDifferentiating gives\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}(x)={z}^{′}(x)+{y}_{0}^{′}(x).\n$$\n\nIn our case, ${y}_{0}(x)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡x.$ Since ${y}_{0}^{′}(x)=-\\operatorname{sin}⁡x$, we have\n\n$$\ny=z+\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\,⇒\\,{y}^{′}={z}^{′}-\\operatorname{sin}⁡x.\n$$\n\nLet's substitute these expressions into our Riccati equation:\n\n$$\n{z}^{′}-\\operatorname{sin}⁡x=2x\\,(z+\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)^{2}-4x\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\,(z+\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)+2x{cos}^{2}⁡x-\\operatorname{sin}⁡x.\n$$\n\nNow, we expand the right-hand side, and simplify:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{z}^{′}-\\operatorname{sin}⁡x & =2x({z}^{2}+2z\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+{cos}^{2}⁡x)-4x\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\,(z+\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)+2x{cos}^{2}⁡x-\\operatorname{sin}⁡x \\\\ {z}^{′}-\\operatorname{sin}⁡x & =2x{z}^{2}-\\operatorname{sin}⁡x \\\\ {z}^{′} & =2x{z}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can solve the obtained equation using separation of variables:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x} & =2x{z}^{2} \\\\ \\frac{1}{{z}^{2}}\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x} & =2x \\\\ \\int \\frac{1}{{z}^{2}}\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int 2x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ \\int {z}^{-2}\\,\\text{d}z & =\\int 2x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ -\\frac{1}{z} & ={x}^{2}+C \\\\ z & =-\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}+C}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $C$ is an arbitrary constant.\n\nFinally, let's substitute $z=y-\\operatorname{cos}⁡x$ to recover the initial variable $y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}z & =-\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}+C} \\\\ y-\\operatorname{cos}⁡x & =-\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}+C} \\\\ y & =-\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}+C}+\\operatorname{cos}⁡x \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡x-\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}+C}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186331628 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "cb23c291-a2fb-5e57-bbc9-7b2c02d0a1ea" :knowledge-point/title "Solving Riccati Differential Equations"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "d01f8519-a7c1-5579-9b2b-6383d999f1f3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=x+7+\\frac{2}{8x-C}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186331630} {:answer/id #uuid "ca4baefc-6278-54f2-b7a6-fc00f881e1a7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=x+7-\\frac{1}{x+C}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186331631} {:answer/id #uuid "dfce43f4-2752-5e81-8a6d-406869b5c8ef" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=x+7+\\frac{1}{8x+C}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186331632} {:answer/id #uuid "406fdfae-660d-5b62-8443-6a44626ef474" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=x+7-\\frac{1}{8x+C}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186331633} {:answer/id #uuid "9129485e-ebac-5604-829f-c6fd564eebab" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=x+7+\\frac{2}{8x+C}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186331634}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "406fdfae-660d-5b62-8443-6a44626ef474" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=x+7-\\frac{1}{8x+C}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186331633} :answer-field/id #uuid "9d36f6f5-c553-5735-b0d7-6580dacae939" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186331629}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "12e73f3b-c5ba-53c5-87de-0da49f5becd7" :question/math-academy-id "q-334137" :question/problem "Given that ${y}_{0}(x)=x+7$ is a particular solution of the Riccati differential equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=8{y}^{2}-16(x+7)y+8(x+7)^{2}+1\n$$\n\nfind the general solution of the equation." :question/worked-solution "The equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=8{y}^{2}-16(x+7)y+8(x+7)^{2}+1,\n$$\n\nis a Riccati differential equation of the form\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=a(x){y}^{2}+b(x)y+c(x),\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n$$\na(x)=8,\\,b(x)=-16(x+7),\\,c(x)=8(x+7)^{2}+1.\n$$\n\nTo reduce the given Riccati equation to a Bernoulli equation, we use the substitution\n\n$$\ny(x)=z(x)+{y}_{0}(x),\n$$\n\nwhere ${y}_{0}(x)$ is a known particular solution.\nDifferentiating gives\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}(x)={z}^{′}(x)+{y}_{0}^{′}(x).\n$$\n\nIn our case, ${y}_{0}(x)=x+7.$ Since ${y}_{0}^{′}(x)=1$, we have\n\n$$\ny=z+x+7\\,⇒\\,{y}^{′}={z}^{′}+1.\n$$\n\nLet's substitute these expressions into our Riccati equation:\n\n$$\n{z}^{′}+1=8(z+x+7)^{2}-16(x+7)(z+x+7)+8(x+7)^{2}+1.\n$$\n\nNow, we expand the right-hand side, and simplify:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{z}^{′}+1 & =8((x+7)^{2}+2(x+7)z+{z}^{2})-16(x+7)(z+x+7)+8(x+7)^{2}+1 \\\\ {z}^{′}+1 & =8{z}^{2}+1 \\\\ {z}^{′} & =8{z}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can solve the obtained equation using separation of variables:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x} & =8{z}^{2} \\\\ \\frac{1}{{z}^{2}}\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x} & =8 \\\\ \\int \\frac{1}{{z}^{2}}\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int 8\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ \\int {z}^{-2}\\,\\text{d}z & =\\int 8\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ -\\frac{1}{z} & =8x+C \\\\ z & =-\\frac{1}{8x+C}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $C$ is an arbitrary constant.\n\nFinally, let's substitute $z=y-(x+7)$ to recover the initial variable $y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}z & =-\\frac{1}{8x+C} \\\\ y-(x+7) & =-\\frac{1}{8x+C} \\\\ y & =-\\frac{1}{8x+C}+x+7 \\\\  & =x+7-\\frac{1}{8x+C}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}]}
